{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Corroborating evidence from a high-concurrency session (2026-08-06/07), which EXTENDS this ticket's scope beyond compact-on-close.\n\nSetup: 6-9 concurrent agents plus the live Jira reconcile bridge, all writing to this one store.\n\nObserved failures, none of them a close/compact:\n1. `rebar comment <id> ...` FAILED outright with `rebar._commands._seam.CommandError: flock: could not acquire lock after 60s`. The comment was silently lost — the command exited and the caller had to re-verify state and retry manually. So the 60s acquire timeout is reachable by ordinary writes, not just by the compact path this ticket names.\n2. Immediately after, `WARNING rebar._store.push: tickets branch push-retry merge skipped: write lock busy; push stays pending`. The push deferred silently; nothing re-announced it (which is the same non-announcement mechanism proven in 2a76 — PUSH_PENDING is only computed on an explicit `rebar fsck`).\n3. `rebar transition` and `rebar edit` routinely took 10-60s+ under this load, and several needed 240-550s timeouts to complete reliably.\n\nWhy this matters for the fix's shape: the ticket currently frames the problem as compact-on-close holding the lock too long. That is the biggest HOLDER, but the failure surface is wider — any writer can exhaust the 60s acquire budget and, when it does, at least one path (`comment`) surfaces it as a hard CommandError while another (push) degrades to a silent WARNING. Two consequences worth folding into the acceptance criteria:\n* a writer that cannot acquire within the budget should not lose the write silently — compare ab54, which records the same class for the COMPLETION_VERDICT sidecar;\n* the acquire budget is currently a fixed 60s that does not scale with contention, so adding agents makes lost writes MORE likely rather than merely slower.\n\nSequential single-agent operation is unaffected; this is specifically the parallel-agent workflow AGENTS.md actively encourages."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786096005824229001,"uuid":"ba72de60-758f-438c-9c54-b68de7e3819c"}